      "id": 1,
      "text": "Randolph was originally called Waterville and was laid out by early settlers in 1856.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randolph%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was renamed Randolph in honor of pioneer settler Gardner Randolph.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randolph%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original downtown sits on an island above the waterline of Tuttle Creek Reservoir, accessible only when Fancy Creek is low.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randolph%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "When Tuttle Creek Reservoir filled in 1962, Randolph was one of four towns entirely submerged, yet the town was rebuilt elsewhere.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randolph%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Locally, the original settlement on the island is called “Old Randolph” by residents.",
